\begin{abstract}
Measurements of extreme-ultraviolet (EUV) coronal loop widths often indicate little or no expansion with height, in apparent contrast to the expected divergence of coronal magnetic fields. We investigate whether this result can be attributed to background emission and instrumental or analysis effects. Synthetic loops with prescribed intensity profiles and expansion factors are embedded in real TRACE EUV images, thereby preserving realistic structured backgrounds, noise, and line-of-sight contamination. The resulting loop widths are recovered using the same procedures applied to observed loops and are compared directly with measurements from real TRACE data. We find that background structure can bias individual width estimates and can obscure weak expansion, particularly for low-contrast loops. However, synthetic loops with substantial expansion remain measurably broader with height under realistic observing conditions. In contrast, the observed loop sample consistently shows approximately constant widths along the visible loop length. These tests indicate that the reported absence of significant EUV loop expansion is not an artifact of background subtraction or image analysis, but is a robust observational property of the loop population studied.
\end{abstract}
